# A FIXED DEFECT, named after it (Go's `test/fixedbugs`), 2026-08-14, panel 048.
#
# SYMPTOM: a **correct** binding missing only its `link` clause was reported like
# this — measured, verbatim:
#
#     internal error: compiling the generated C failed:
#     Undefined symbols for architecture arm64:
#       "_sqlite3_libversion_number", referenced from: _h_nolink_main
#     the generated C is at build/2a141c4fbafbf91d/nolink.c
#     EXIT=2
#
# Five things wrong with it, and the panel's ffi-pragmatist counted them against
# this file's own module doc: the compiler blames **itself**; exit **2** says the
# tool could not run when the input has a diagnostic; there is no `.hero` line
# and no span; it invites the author to debug generated C under `build/<hash>/`
# for a three-line `extern` group; and the word `link` never appears — while
# "compiling the generated C failed" is wrong, because it was the **linker**.
#
# CAUSE: `emit/ffi.rs::explain` turned three classes of clang's verdict into
# Heroes diagnostics and had no fourth for the linker's, so `compile.rs` took the
# `internal error` path. It is the same shape §4.19's return assertion had before
# panel 036, and this file's own header already carried the verdict on it.
#
# FIX: `ffi_missing_link`, exit 1, on the author's line, naming the form.
#
# **THE GATE IS THE POINT.** The class is admitted only when the undefined symbol
# is one *this program* declared in an `extern` group — `declaration()`, a fact
# about the value. An undefined symbol nobody declared is the **emitter's** bug
# (a generated `hash` prototyped and never defined is a recorded instance) and
# stays exit 2, where it belongs. `extern` names are the only unmangled names in
# the unit (CLAUDE.md §7), so `sqlite3_libversion_number` against `h_ffimissinglink_main` is a
# perfect discriminator.
#
# **No fix is offered**, and four judges agree why: the repair is `link "<name>"`
# and the compiler does not know the name. A header-to-library table would be
# wrong on three rows measured — `-lrt` is an error on Darwin, `-liconv` is
# required on Darwin and does not exist on glibc, `pthread` moved into libc at
# glibc 2.34 — which is CLAUDE.md §11's expiring premise written as an algorithm.
# The ffi-pragmatist vetoed exactly that.
#
# **The library is `sqlite3`, and choosing it took two tries.** Not `math.h`: on
# Darwin arm64 `sqrt` is lowered to a hardware instruction at every level and
# never reaches the linker at all, so a libm case passes here for the wrong
# reason (measured, panel 048). And not `iconv`, which was the first choice and
# was wrong for a reason the panel's own ffi-pragmatist had already reported and
# this file had already quoted: `iconv_open` is **in libc on glibc** and needs
# `-liconv` only on Darwin. It is the one row in its table that is *inverted*
# between the two platforms, which makes it the worst possible case for a test
# that must fail on both. The CI said so within three minutes.
#
# A third-party library is the shape that agrees everywhere — the pragmatist's
# table has `sqlite3`, `curl` and `z` as the only rows where the library name is
# platform-independent, which is also why panel 047's prediction 2 held.
# Bulk regression case.

extern "sqlite3.h"
    function sqlite3_libversion_number() -> i64      #~ ffi_missing_link

function main()
    print("sqlite ", sqlite3_libversion_number())
